questions.modelAnswer:
class RigettiBackend(QuantumBackend):
    def __init__(self, device_name: str):
        self._device_name = device_name
        self._client = rigetti_client.Client(device_name)

    def run(self, circuit, shots: int) -> dict:
        result = self._client.submit(circuit, shots=shots)
        return result.get_counts()

    def name(self) -> str:
        return f'rigetti({self._device_name})'

This satisfies the shared contract identically to the LocalSimulatorBackend and IBMHardwareBackend implementations, so algorithm code calling backend.run(qc, shots=1024) works unmodified regardless of which concrete backend is injected. get_calibration_data() should NOT be added to the shared interface because not every backend can meaningfully support it — a classical simulator has no real calibration data to report, and forcing it into the shared contract means either faking a meaningless return value for the simulator or breaking the 'every backend implements every method the same way' guarantee the interface exists to provide. This is capability leakage: a hardware-specific feature polluting a contract meant to be uniformly satisfiable. The fix is a separate, optional interface (or a Rigetti-specific subclass) that algorithm code only reaches for when it explicitly knows it's targeting real hardware.

